//! AX / JAS audio driver: audio-DMA registers, AI/DSP setup, and the JAS frame pump.
//!
//! # What lives here
//!
//! This module is the reader-facing home for the low-level audio driver in
//! `main.dol`. It covers twenty-two clusters:
//!
//! 1. **Audio-DMA register accessors** (`0x8019F180..0x8019F1E0`) - the four
//!    original functions that start the channel and read its state back.
//! 2. **AI/DSP bring-up and the DSP interrupt hook** (`0x8019F0B0..0x8019F420`)
//!    - program the DMA source/length, install the interrupt handler, and walk
//!    the AI control block at `0xCD006C00`.
//! 3. **JAS output driver** (`0x801A0CA0..0x801A1FC8`) - callback slots, the
//!    two-block scratch area, driver defaults, the per-frame mix/DMA kick, and
//!    the DMA-completion and frame-boundary entry points.
//! 4. **JAS object pools, output ring and parameters** (`0x8019B660`,
//!    `0x801A0050..0x801A3820`) - wave 4.
//! 5. **JAS voice note/buffer control and JAI timer queue**
//!    (`0x801A31A0..0x801ABB40`) - wave 5. This covers the per-voice sample
//!    buffer sets, their allocation/clear/free hooks, the 2-, 3- and 4-buffer
//!    start/retry/stop paths, and the JAI sequence timer list.
//! 6. **Game BRSAR cue and bank-group hooks** (`0x8019B880..0x8019D1C0`) -
//!    wave 18. The sound-manager methods that select cues from the loaded
//!    BRSAR archive, mute or enable bank groups, and drive the BGM stream.
//! 7. **Sound-manager lifecycle, sequence handle and DSP buffers**
//!    (`0x8019B1D0..0x801A08D0`) - wave 22. The constructor/reset pair around
//!    the cue hooks, the shared sequence handle and its cue-table selectors,
//!    the fixed-constant BPM helper, and the six DSP buffer address getters.
//! 8. **Remaining AX/JAS/AI glue** (`0x8019DF80..0x801AB5B0`) - wave 27. The
//!    two companion cue tables, the reverse-bank DSP selectors, the JAS level
//!    getters/setters, the voice counter/limit/table accessors, and the JAI
//!    voice-slot and periodic-timer wrappers.
//! 9. **Sound-manager stream and sequence transport**
//!    (`0x80194DF0..0x80196830`) - wave 29. The BGM-stream start/stop gates,
//!    the sequence-handle release helpers, the stream-enable primitive and the
//!    sequence-status queries that sit under the wave-18 cue hooks.
//! 10. **Sequence dispatch, stream records and JAI timer records**
//!    (`0x80195300..0x801ABA80`) - wave 31. The shared sequence-operation
//!    dispatcher and its four pair operators, the stream-position bitmask,
//!    the stream-record latch, and the JAI voice-slot and timer-record
//!    accessors.
//! 11. **JAS DSP block pump and voice kernels** (`0x801A08F0..0x801A93F0`) -
//!    wave 90. The three-bank DSP block pump with its command builder, the
//!    96-voice table initialiser, the pan-curve helper, the 2-/3-/4-tap mixer
//!    state builders, the 3- and 4-channel 8-bit resamplers, the float effect
//!    mixers and the 3-/4-channel FIR resamplers.
//! 12. **`0x801A` bucket tail: JAI block lists and kernel helpers**
//!    (`0x801A9C90..0x801AFDB0`) - wave 92. The JAI block-list unlink, the
//!    arena/allocator words around the JAI block table, the DSP halt path and
//!    the kernel/cache/charset leaves that follow the audio span.
//! 13. **`0x8019` bucket: cue dispatch, stage helpers and probes**
//!    (`0x80194DA0..0x8019F050`) - wave 93. The four vtable cue-dispatch
//!    wrappers, the stream stage/fade helpers that share the `+0x91C..+0x92C`
//!    block, the cue-object constructor/destructor pair, the four
//!    stream-presence probes and the two interpolation leaves below the DSP
//!    bring-up.
//! 14. **`0x801A` bucket tail: SPR accessors, boot-info probes and argv
//!    marshalling** (`0x801A9AF0..0x801AFEF0`) - wave 98. The JAS hook
//!    getter/setter pair, the ten special-purpose-register accessors behind
//!    the performance-monitor block, `OSGetConsoleType` with its two console
//!    tables, the two arena write-back helpers, the console/banner report, the
//!    exception-vector bring-up, the Hollywood register/timing step, the
//!    context dump, the two `argv` marshallers, the video-mode setter and the
//!    two `OSExec` leaves.
//! 15. **`0x8019` bucket: stream-stage state machine and cue-list walkers**
//!    (`0x801955F0..0x8019ECE0`) - wave 99. The stage fade/countdown updater,
//!    the two-stream level applier, the cue-name group writer, the status
//!    probe, the eight-byte row allocators, the four-list token/ID release
//!    walkers, the cue-table destructor, the BRSAR row publishers, the cue
//!    lookup/start wrappers, the cue-state group-volume switch, the sequence
//!    start helper and the fade/mask record applier.
//! 16. **`0x801A` bucket tail: the two remaining float effect mixers and the
//!    boot-stage leaves** (`0x801A3880..0x801AF5D4`) - wave 101. The 3- and
//!    4-channel delay/filter mixers below the wave-90 resamplers, the
//!    `0x80`-byte segment reload, the `OSContext` resume patch, the IOS boot
//!    probe and the hand-off that installs the wave-92/98 context and switches
//!    stacks into `0x801AF7C0`.
//! 17. **`0x801A` bucket tail: the OSContext register save and the boot-stage
//!    stack helpers** (`0x801AC710..0x801AE050`) - wave 103. The two SPR 922/923
//!    descriptor writers, the full GPR/GQR `OSContext` snapshot, the
//!    stack-pointer reader, the two `call on an alternate stack` helpers and the
//!    cache-flush tail jump into the freshly loaded stage.
//! 18. **`0x8019` bucket: the last nineteen entries** (`0x80195510..0x8019F420`)
//!    - wave 104. The cue/envelope and tempo leaves, the two player-SE spawn
//!    twins, the cue-record publishers, the cue-state transition, the position
//!    matcher, the level/pan ramp, the 2,052-byte scalar-record table and the
//!    boot callback trampoline. `0x8019F490` was the one entry this pass left
//!    open (its export is one call plus an endless loop); wave 114 lands it in
//!    cluster 22.
//! 19. **`0x801A` bucket close-out: FPU context and the boot-stage loader**
//!    (`0x801ACAD0..0x801AF7C0`) - wave 105. The FPU save/restore trio, the
//!    two `OSExec` stages, the proportional-font blitter and the YUV
//!    framebuffer/banner bring-up. The six performance-monitor leaves stay
//!    owned by the `runtime::os` range row and `0x801A9CE0` only writes a
//!    non-volatile FPR, so none of those seven entries got a body in that pass.
//! 20. **`0x801A` bucket close-out: the FPU non-IEEE-mode leaf**
//!    (`0x801A9CE0`) - wave 108. The eight-byte `mtfsb1 29; blr` sets
//!    FPSCR[`NI`], so the wave-105 reading of `0x801A9CE0` as a dead
//!    non-volatile FPR write is revised here.
//! 21. **`0x801A` bucket close-out: the six performance-monitor SPR leaves**
//!    (`0x801A9BC0..0x801A9C10`) - wave 109. The six one-instruction `mtspr`
//!    bodies fill the gap left by the `runtime::os` `clear_performance_monitor`
//!    range row, which had only reached these addresses as opaque callees. With
//!    them the `0x801A0000` bucket has a byte-level body for all 271 inventory
//!    entries.
//! 22. **`0x8019` bucket close-out: the AI DMA calibration loop**
//!    (`0x8019F490`) - wave 114. Ghidra exports this 460-byte body as one call
//!    followed by an endless loop, so the wave-104 pass skipped it. The
//!    instruction words are intact in the ELF, and the translated body does
//!    what they do: two AI sample-counter/time-base measurements, the
//!    five-scale window classifier and the settle delay. With it the
//!    `0x80190000` bucket has a byte-level body for all 241 inventory entries.
//!
//! `AX` is the low-level DSP voice API; `JAS` is the NintendoWare sound driver
//! that feeds it. The functions below are the plumbing between the JAS mixer
//! and the audio hardware, not the sequence player (`audio::seq`).
//!
//! ## Audio-DMA accessors
//!
//! | address | Rust function | role |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | `0x8019F180` | [`fn_8019f180`] | set the DMA start bit |
//! | `0x8019F1A0` | [`fn_8019f1a0`] | bytes left in the current transfer |
//! | `0x8019F1B0` | [`fn_8019f1b0`] | programmed source address |
//! | `0x8019F1D0` | [`fn_8019f1d0`] | programmed transfer length |
//!
//! # Original address range
//!
//! `0x8019B1D0..0x801ABDF0` across adjacent driver/sequence bands. The
//! original four accessors were promoted from the staging lane
//! `src/subsystems/r80180_801c0.rs` (range `0x80180000..0x801C0000`); the
//! wave-3 additions come directly from the `0x8019`/`0x801A` export files.
//! Wave 4 extends the range with the JAS object pools and ring helpers
//! (`0x801A0050..0x801A3820`) and the game sound/BRSAR state transition at
//! `0x8019B660`. Wave 5 adds the JAS voice buffer control block
//! (`0x801A31A0..0x801A72F0`) and the JAI timer queue
//! (`0x801AB9E0..0x801ABB40`, with the shared unlinker in `runtime::os`).
//! Wave 11 adds the 96-sample sequence resamplers (`0x801A8400..0x801A9990`),
//! the per-frame voice cost pass (`0x801A0380..0x801A3DB0`) and the JAI block
//! pool (`0x801ABD80..0x801ABDF0`). Wave 18 adds the game sound-manager cue
//! and bank-group hooks (`0x8019B880..0x8019D1C0`), so the covered span is now
//! `0x8019B880..0x801ABDF0`; it is not contiguous because the wave-18 band
//! starts below the DSP bring-up. Wave 22 extends the lower edge with the
//! sound-object lifecycle (`0x8019B1D0`) and the DSP buffer selectors
//! (`0x801A06D0..0x801A08D0`), making the overall span
//! `0x8019B1D0..0x801ABDF0`. Wave 27 adds the companion cue tables
//! (`0x8019DF80..0x8019DFB0`), the reverse-bank DSP selectors
//! (`0x801A0730..0x801A0870`), the remaining JAS level and voice accessors
//! (`0x801A18C0..0x801A3360`) and the JAI voice-slot and periodic-timer
//! wrappers (`0x801AB020..0x801AB5B0`). Wave 29 adds the sound-manager
//! stream/sequence transport band (`0x80194DF0..0x80196830`). Wave 31 fills
//! the adjacent sequence-dispatch wrappers (`0x80195300..0x801954A0`), the stream-position
//! and transition helpers (`0x801969B0..0x80196B90`), the JAS descriptor
//! selectors (`0x801A9AA0..0x801A9AB0`) and the JAI timer-record accessors
//! (`0x801AB040..0x801ABA80`), so the overall span is now
//! `0x80194DF0..0x801ABDF0`, still with gaps outside this lane. Wave 90 fills
//! the DSP kernel band `0x801A08F0..0x801A93F0`, which lies inside that span.
//! Wave 92 adds the `0x801A` bucket tail (`0x801A9C90..0x801AFDB0`), so the
//! overall span is now `0x80194DF0..0x801AFDB0`, still with the same internal
//! gaps; the new band is kernel/allocator code rather than mixer code. Wave 93
//! fills the `0x8019` cue/stage band (`0x80194DA0..0x8019F050`), which sits
//! below the wave-29 transport code and above the DSP bring-up. Wave 98 fills
//! the remaining `0x801A9AF0..0x801AFEF0` run of the `0x801A` bucket: the
//! special-purpose-register setters, the console-type probe and its report
//! helpers, the exception bring-up and the `OSExec` argument/video helpers.
//! That run lies above the wave-90 DSP kernels and overlaps the wave-92 tail
//! only at the `0x801A9C90` barrier, so the overall span is unchanged. Wave 103
//! adds the last reconstructible leaves of the same bucket
//! (`0x801AC710..0x801AE050`): the SPR 922/923 descriptor pair, the
//! `OSContext` GPR snapshot and the boot-stage stack/flush helpers. It sits
//! inside the wave-98 run, so the overall span is still
//! `0x80194DF0..0x801AFDB0`. Wave 104 closes the `0x80190000` bucket with
//! `0x80195510..0x8019F420`: the tempo/cue leaves, the two 900-byte player-SE
//! spawn twins, the GQR-prologue bodies whose `psq_l` tails are unreachable,
//! the 2,052-byte scalar-record table and the boot callback trampoline. That
//! run lies inside the wave-93/99 span, so the overall span is unchanged.
//! Wave 105 lands the last seven reconstructible bodies of the `0x801A0000`
//! bucket (`0x801ACAD0..0x801AF7C0`): the FPU context load/save trio, the two
//! `OSExec` stages, the proportional-font blitter and the YUV framebuffer and
//! banner bring-up. Those addresses sit inside the wave-92/98 run, so the
//! overall span is still `0x80194DF0..0x801AFDB0`. Wave 108 adds the single
//! remaining leaf of the same bucket, `0x801A9CE0` (`mtfsb1 29`, the FPSCR
//! non-IEEE-mode bit); it sits inside the wave-92 run as well, so the overall
//! span is unchanged. Wave 109 adds the six performance-monitor leaves
//! (`0x801A9BC0..0x801A9C10`), which lie in the same run, so the overall span
//! is still `0x80194DF0..0x801AFDB0`.
//!
