//! ## Wave 103 - `OSContext` register save and boot-stage stack helpers
//! (`0x801AC710..0x801AE050`)
//!
//! The `0x801A0000` bucket closed at 271 inventory entries. Twenty-one were
//! still unclaimed after wave 101: six are the performance-monitor clears that
//! `runtime::os` already owns as a range row, four are the `OSExec`/font/video
//! bodies reserved for a later pass, three are paired-single `GQR` save/restore
//! routines, and the remaining eight are the leaves below. Wave 103 lands seven
//! of them; the eighth (`0x801A9CE0`) touches only FPU state and was left
//! unclaimed until wave 108. See `docs/COVERAGE_WAVE103_AX.md`.
//!
//! | address | Rust function | role | confidence |
//! |---|---|---|---|
//! | `0x801AC710` | [`fn_801ac710`] | write the SPR 922/923 descriptor pair with flag `0x12` | medium |
//! | `0x801AC740` | [`fn_801ac740`] | write the SPR 922/923 descriptor pair with flag `0x02` | medium |
//! | `0x801ACDB0` | [`fn_801acdb0`] | snapshot `r13..r31`, the GQRs, CR/LR/MSR/CTR/XER into an `OSContext` | high |
//! | `0x801ACF10` | [`fn_801acf10`] | return the current stack pointer | high |
//! | `0x801ACF20` | [`fn_801acf20`] | run a callback on an alternate stack, then unwind | high |
//! | `0x801ACF50` | [`fn_801acf50`] | the same switch with the callback in `r7` and the stack in `r8` | medium |
//! | `0x801AE050` | [`fn_801ae050`] | enable the I-cache, `sync`/`isync`, then jump to a stage entry | medium |
//!
//! The `OSContext` layout used by [`fn_801acdb0`] is the one that
//! [`crate::runtime::os::clear_context`] and
//! [`crate::runtime::os::init_context`] already document: `+0x04` stack,
//! `+0x08` `r2`, `+0x0C` the live-context marker, `+0x34..+0x7C`
//! `r13..r31`, `+0x80` CR, `+0x84` LR, `+0x88` CTR, `+0x8C` XER,
//! `+0x90..+0x188` `f0..f31`, `+0x190` FPSCR, `+0x198`/`+0x19C` SRR0/SRR1,
//! `+0x1A0`/`+0x1A2` the mode/state halfwords and `+0x1A8..+0x1C0`
//! `GQR1..GQR7`. Nothing in this pass writes that block except the snapshot
//! itself.
//!
//! ## Wave 105 - the `0x801A0000` bucket close-out
//!
//! The bucket held 271 inventory entries. After wave 103 fourteen were still
//! unclaimed; this pass lands seven and explains the other seven. The six
//! `0x801A9BC0..0x801A9C10` `mtspr` leaves were left to the `runtime::os`
//! `clear_performance_monitor` range row, and `0x801A9CE0` looked like an
//! eight-byte leaf whose only non-branch word writes a non-volatile FPR. Wave
//! 108 decoded that word as `mtfsb1 29` and lands it. Wave 109 revisits the six
//! `mtspr` leaves: the range row reaches them only as opaque callees, so the
//! bytes themselves still had no body. That pass adds one body per leaf and
//! closes the `0x801A0000` bucket at the instruction level.
//!
//! | address | Rust function | role | confidence |
//! |---|---|---|---|
//! | `0x801ACAD0` | [`fn_801acad0`] | restore the FPU registers from an `OSContext` | medium |
//! | `0x801ACC00` | [`fn_801acc00`] | save the FPU registers into an `OSContext` | medium |
//! | `0x801AD3B0` | [`fn_801ad3b0`] | enable MSR[FP], then save the FPU registers | medium |
//! | `0x801AE0E0` | [`fn_801ae0e0`] | first-stage `OSExec` image picker and reader | medium |
//! | `0x801AE840` | [`fn_801ae840`] | DVD/argv setup and the compressed-stage hand-off | medium |
//! | `0x801AF180` | [`fn_801af180`] | proportional-font string blitter | medium |
//! | `0x801AF7C0` | [`fn_801af7c0`] | YUV framebuffer fill, banner and halt | medium |
//!
//! The context blocks use the layout documented above plus `+0x1C8..+0x2C4`
//! for the 32 paired-single slots. Both save bodies write exactly `0x1D0` bytes
//! of register state; the load body restores it. `HID2[PS]` must be enabled for
//! the `psq_l`/`psq_st` half, which is why the original guard reads only the
//! `+0x1A2` state bit.
//!
//! # Why it exists
//!
//! The audio hardware consumes one 0x180-byte block at a time through the
//! audio DMA. The AI/DSP block (`0xCD006C00`) gates the DSP, while the DSP
//! mailbox (`0xCC005000`) is used by the JAS mixer to upload a scratch block
//! and to wait for completion. These functions are kept together so the AX/JAS
//! layer can be followed from the register write to the frame pump without
//! reading the address-range staging files.
//!
//! # Register map
//!
//! Dolphin and libogc call the `0xCC005000` block the `__DSPRegs` map and the
//! `0xCD006C00` block the AI (audio-interface) map. The names below are
//! inferred from those mappings, not from strings in the binary.
//!
//! | address | width | meaning |
//! |---|---|---|
//! | `0xCC005000` | 16 | DSP mailbox, high half of the command/argument word |
//! | `0xCC005002` | 16 | DSP mailbox, low half |
//! | `0xCC00500A` | 16 | DSP control/status; [`fn_8019f370`] clears 5/7 and sets 3 |
//! | `0xCC005030` | 16 | audio-DMA source address high; bits 0..12 are address bits 16..28 |
//! | `0xCC005032` | 16 | audio-DMA source address low; bits 5..15 are address bits 5..15 |
//! | `0xCC005036` | 16 | bits 0..14 = length in 32-byte blocks, bit 15 = start |
//! | `0xCC00503A` | 16 | bits 0..14 = remaining 32-byte blocks |
//! | `0xCD006C00` | 32 | AI control; bits 5, 6 and 7 are tested or written |
//! | `0xCD006C04` | 32 | AI volume, written to zero during init |
//! | `0xCD006C0C` | 32 | AI interrupt timing, written to zero during init |
//! | `0x800000F8` | 32 | OS bus-clock constant used to derive the timer scales |
//!
//! # Dependencies
//!
//! [`crate::platform`] - big-endian volatile reads/writes and absolute calls.
//! [`crate::boot::entry::SDA1_BASE`] supplies the `r13` base (`0x8042F680`);
//! every `r13 - N` access below is written as `SDA1_BASE - N`.
//!
//! The functions are exact PowerPC/volatile translations and are **not
//! host-safe**; running them requires the platform helpers to be backed by an
//! emulator or a mock MMIO map.
//!
//! # Cross-lane callees (opaque here)
//!
//! * `0x801B1140` / `0x801B1180` - interrupt disable/restore.
//! * `0x801B11B0` / `0x801B1590` - install interrupt handler / unmask.
//! * `0x801ACF80` / `0x801ACD40` - OSContext clear / set-current pair.
//! * `0x801AC470` / `0x801AC440` - data-cache flush / invalidate.
//! * `0x800046B4` - `memset`-shaped helper (Ghidra loses its arguments).
//! * `0x801B5F80` - `OSGetTime` (64-bit time base).
//! * `0x801D5720` / `0x801D56E0` - DSP mailbox write / busy-bit read.
//! * `0x801D5800` / `0x801D5740` / `0x801D5810` - DSP queue status, reset and
//!   queue (used by the task descriptor in [`fn_801a1ce0`]).
//! * `0x801B4B20` - initialise the DSP task's synchronisation object.
//! * `0x80277A90` / `0x80278780` / `0x8027A7D0` - BRSAR bank-group load,
//!   lookup and parameter write (game sound hook [`fn_8019b660`]).
//! * `0x8019B4F0`, `0x80196600`, `0x80195CB0`, `0x8019BAF0`,
//!   `0x80195F10`, `0x8019E080` - game sound-system reset, query, parameter,
//!   fade and state-apply helpers.
//! * `0x801954A0`, `0x80195820`, `0x80195E80`, `0x80195FB0`, `0x80196010`,
//!   `0x80196050`, `0x80196550`, `0x801965B0`, `0x80196BD0` - the sound
//!   system's own cue/stream/volume primitives used by the wave-18 hooks.
//! * `0x8019E2E0`, `0x8019E4C0`, `0x8019E6F0`, `0x8019E8E0`, `0x8019E940` -
//!   sequence-handle helpers (track mute, cue apply, envelope step).
//! * `0x8019CA00`, `0x8019D2D0` - cue restart and per-group re-apply; both are
//!   translated in wave 99. `0x8019D480` (the player-SE volume table) is still
//!   opaque.
//! * `0x80279D50`, `0x8027A410`, `0x8027A850` - BRSAR "is playing", archive
//!   handle capture and per-group volume write.
//! * `0x802D6E00` - engine pause/audio-duck switch (`1` while a cue owns the
//!   audio, `0` when it releases it).
//!
//! # Related original code (not translated here)
//!
//! * `0x801A0E90` - the JAS mixer that fills a 0x180-byte DMA block.
//! * `0x801A08F0` - large per-frame helper.
//! * `0x801AC350` - clears the same DMA start bit outside this band.
//!
//! # Start here
//!
//! [`fn_8019f1b0`] shows how the 32-bit source address is split across two
//! halfword registers; [`fn_8019f100`] is the writer for that pair.
//! [`fn_801a1dd0`] is the complete JAS driver bring-up, and
//! [`fn_801a1ba0`] is the DMA-completion path that advances the ring.
//! For the wave-4 object pools start at [`fn_801a0050`]; for the 16-bit
//! output ring start at [`fn_801a2020`], and for the BRSAR hook at
//! [`fn_8019b660`]. For wave 5 start at [`fn_801a31a0`] for voice parameters,
//! [`fn_801a55f0`] for the 3-buffer sample set, [`fn_801a70a0`] for the voice
//! buffer table, and [`fn_801ab9e0`] for the JAI timer queue. For wave 18
//! start at [`fn_8019b880`] for cue selection and [`fn_8019c880`] for the
//! bank-group mute/unmute path. For wave 27 start at [`fn_801a0730`] for the
//! reverse DSP bank selectors, [`fn_801a2180`] for the parameter block, and
//! [`fn_801ab020`] for the JAI voice-slot table. For wave 92 start at
//! [`fn_801abcf0`] for the JAI block list and [`fn_801ac350`] for the DSP halt
//! path. For wave 93 start at [`fn_80197300`] for the cue object and
//! [`fn_80195010`] for the four vtable dispatch wrappers. For wave 99 start at
//! [`fn_801955f0`] for the stage fade block and [`fn_8019ca00`] for the
//! cue-state group-volume switch. For wave 104 start at [`fn_80196bd0`] for the
//! tempo conversion, [`fn_80197f00`] for the player-SE spawn and
//! [`fn_8019d480`] for the scalar-record table. For wave 114 start at
//! [`fn_8019f490`] for the AI DMA calibration loop that [`fn_8019f1f0`] runs
//! during AI bring-up.
//!
//! # Glossary
//!
//! * **AX** - Nintendo's audio library for the GameCube/Wii DSP.
//! * **JAS** - NintendoWare's sound driver layer above AX; it mixes voices and
//!   feeds the audio DMA.
//! * **JAI** - the JAS interpreter/sequence layer. Wave 5 translates its timer
//!   list and the JAS voice buffers it schedules.
//! * **AI** - the audio interface block at `0xCD006C00` that gates the DSP.
//! * **DSP** - the audio digital signal processor behind `0xCC005000`.
//! * **DMA** - the 32-byte-block engine that moves mixed audio to the DSP.
//! * **SDA** - the PowerPC small-data areas. `r13` is [`SDA1_BASE`]
//!   (`0x8042F680`) and `r2` is [`SDA2_BASE`] (`0x80433080`); wave-5 float
//!   constants are `r2`-relative.
//! * **MMIO** - memory-mapped hardware registers in the uncached window.
//! * **DOL** - the Wii executable container (`sys/main.dol`); this code lives
//!   in its text segment.
//!
//! # Confidence summary
//!
//! The four original accessors and nine wave-3 functions have exact observed
//! arithmetic (`high`). Nine are `medium` because a callee identity, a field
//! role (AI control bits, JAS ring/MODE meaning), or the DSP mailbox protocol
//! is inferred. No function is a stub; every call and every observable write is
//! accounted for, and unknowns are marked `// unknown:` or `// inferred:`.
//!
//! Wave 4 adds 23 functions: 20 are `high` (all stores and loop bounds are
//! explicit) and 3 are `medium` (`0x801A00E0` and `0x801A1CE0` infer a
//! destructor/callback role, `0x8019B660` infers the vtable and BRSAR return
//! conventions). None is a stub.
//!
//! Wave 5 adds 24 functions: 19 are `high` (all buffer offsets, loop bounds and
//! interrupt brackets are observed) and 5 are `medium` (`0x801A34F0`,
//! `0x801A55F0`, `0x801A5E90`, `0x801A60C0` and `0x801A67A0`) because the
//! allocator hooks, unnamed `r2` float limits and the float-to-int helper are
//! inferred. None is a stub. The shared unlinker `0x801AB640` is
//! [`crate::runtime::os::cancel_alarm`].
//!
//! Wave 11 adds 22 functions: 16 are `high` (every offset, flag bit and
//! fixed-point product is explicit) and 6 are `medium` (`0x801A2B10`,
//! `0x801A8410`, `0x801A8C10`, `0x801A8F30`, `0x801A9780` and `0x801ABD80`)
//! because the per-frame cost base, the allocator hook, the unnamed `r2`
//! float limits and one return convention are inferred. None is a stub.
//!
//! Wave 18 adds 23 functions: 21 are `high` (the state guards, loop bounds,
//! flag bits and cue ids are explicit in the Ghidra C) and 2 are `medium`
//! (`0x8019B880` infers the bank-group parameter role, `0x8019BBA0` infers
//! the manager vtable slot and the archive-handle SDA word). None is a stub.
//!
//! Wave 22 adds 25 functions: 17 are `high` (every store, table bound and
//! buffer stride is explicit) and 8 are `medium` (`0x8019B1E0`, `0x8019B440`,
//! `0x8019B4A0`, `0x8019DC90`, `0x8019DE30`, `0x8019DEC0`, `0x8019DF50` and
//! `0x8019F090`) because a base-constructor role, an allocator convention, a
//! seven-word query record and two SDA2 default words are inferred. None is a
//! stub.
//!
//! Wave 27 adds 25 functions: 24 are `high` (one or two explicit SDA/table
//! accesses, or a simple guard and call) and 1 is `medium` (`0x801AB5B0`)
//! because the 64-bit timer base and the register-save thunk are modelled
//! explicitly. None is a stub.
//!
//! Wave 31 adds 22 functions: 14 are `high` (explicit guards, call order and
//! timer fields) and 8 are `medium` because a virtual-call argument list, the
//! stream-record layout, or one dropped callee argument is inferred. None is a
//! stub.
//!
//! Wave 90 adds 14 functions: 0 are `high` and 14 are `medium` because every
//! one of them is a DSP kernel or mixer-state builder whose unnamed `sdata2`
//! limits, coefficient roles and (in two cases) plane strides are inferred from
//! the surrounding code; the guards, table offsets, calls and stores are
//! literal. None is a stub.
//!
//! Wave 92 adds 28 functions: 22 are `high` (bare `blr`/`sync` leaves, single
//! SDA reads or writes, single-register decodes and the literal hex/charset
//! arithmetic) and 6 are `medium` (`0x801AB1C0`, `0x801ABCF0`, `0x801AC350`,
//! `0x801AC770`, `0x801AC810` and `0x801AC820`) because a varargs call shape,
//! one dropped `r4`, the `HID2` cache fields, or the unreachable tail of the
//! DSP halt path is not fully recoverable. One row is `partial`:
//! `0x801AC350` reproduces the exported halt path, whose last three blocks were
//! dropped by Ghidra as unreachable. None is a stub.
//!
//! Wave 93 adds 28 functions: 6 are `high` (the constant `1` leaf, the
//! `+0x24` store, the cue destructor, the two-branch publish helper, the
//! mode-band query and the empty `blr` leaf) and 22 are `medium` because the
//! four vtable wrapper argument roles, the dropped operands of the stream
//! pokes (`0x80266440`, `0x80267420`, `0x802610C0`), the lazy-create
//! receiver, the `sdata2` level/bound roles and the single-precision calling
//! convention of the float-first helpers are inferred. None is a stub.
//!
//! Wave 99 adds 27 functions: 3 are `high` (the 14-element cue-table
//! destructor, the cue-state level/envelope switch and the four-byte
//! `0x8019E800` tail thunk) and 24 are `medium` because the BRSAR list node
//! layout, the `sdata2` level roles, the dropped operands of the `0x802667D0`
//! and `0x802674B0` call pairs, and the then-untranslated `0x80195510` body
//! are inferred. None is a stub.
//!
//! Wave 104 adds 18 functions: 0 are `high` and 18 are `medium` because every
//! body depends on at least one unnamed `sdata2` word, an inferred cue-record
//! field, or a dropped call operand. The six bodies that carry an
//! `unaff_GQR0`/`ldexpf` prologue in the export (`0x8019A6D0`, `0x8019B220`,
//! `0x8019BED0`, `0x8019C370`, `0x8019D480`, `0x8019EDB0`) are translated from
//! their reachable float arithmetic; the `psq_l` tail that produced the
//! prologue is one of Ghidra's removed unreachable blocks. `0x8019F490` was the
//! only skip of the bucket at the time; wave 114 reconstructs it from the
//! instruction words. None is a stub.
//!
//! Wave 105 adds 7 functions: 0 are `high` and 7 are `medium`. The three FPU
//! context bodies need the paired-single/GQR form and a base register Ghidra
//! leaves unnamed; the two `OSExec` bodies infer the IOS service split, the
//! decompressor entry triple and the shift word; the font blitter infers the
//! 24x24 nibble-plane layout; and the framebuffer painter infers thirteen
//! `sdata2` coefficient roles and the font-record height field. Every call,
//! guard, byte size and store is literal. None is a stub.
//!
//! Wave 114 adds 1 function: 0 are `high` and 1 is `medium`. `0x8019F490` is
//! translated from the machine code because its export is degenerate; every
//! instruction word, mask, spin condition and branch target is literal, while
//! the AI control bit roles and the loop's purpose are inferred from
//! [`fn_8019f1f0`], which writes the five `(scratch:scale)` pairs it reads.
//! None is a stub.

use crate::boot::entry::{SDA1_BASE, SDA2_BASE};
use crate::platform;
use crate::platform::{
    read_f32, read_i32, read_u16, read_u32, read_u8, write_f32, write_u16, write_u32, write_u8,
};

/// `0xCC005030` - audio-DMA source address, high 16 bits.
/// `// inferred:` register name from the Dolphin/libogc `__DSPRegs` mapping.
const AUDIO_DMA_START_HI: usize = 0xCC00_5030;
/// `0xCC005032` - audio-DMA source address, low 16 bits.
const AUDIO_DMA_START_LO: usize = 0xCC00_5032;
/// `0xCC005036` - audio-DMA length in 32-byte blocks; bit 15 starts the DMA.
const AUDIO_DMA_CONTROL_LEN: usize = 0xCC00_5036;
/// `0xCC00503A` - audio-DMA bytes left, in units of 32 bytes.
const AUDIO_DMA_BYTES_LEFT: usize = 0xCC00_503A;

/// Starts the DSP audio DMA by setting bit 15 of the control/length register.
///
/// This is the "enable" half of the two accessors: `0x801AC350` clears the same
/// bit to stop the channel. The length itself is programmed by [`fn_8019f100`]
/// from `len >> 5`.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_8019f180` at `0x8019f180` (20 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/8019f180_FUN_8019f180.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// None.
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// Read-modify-writes the 16-bit MMIO register at `0xCC005036`.
///
/// # Safety
/// Only meaningful on Wii hardware or an emulator that maps the DSP block.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - exact set is observed; role inferred from the Dolphin register map.
pub unsafe fn fn_8019f180() {
    write_u16(
        AUDIO_DMA_CONTROL_LEN,
        read_u16(AUDIO_DMA_CONTROL_LEN) | 0x8000,
    );
}

/// Returns the number of bytes left in the current audio DMA.
///
/// The hardware counts in 32-byte blocks in the low 15 bits; the function
/// shifts by five to convert to bytes. Bit 15 is masked off.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_8019f1a0` at `0x8019f1a0` (16 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/8019f1a0_FUN_8019f1a0.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// None.
///
/// # Returns
/// Remaining bytes, i.e. `(0xCC00503A & 0x7FFF) << 5`.
///
/// # Side effects
/// None; MMIO read-only.
///
/// # Safety
/// Only meaningful on hardware/emulator with the DSP register block mapped.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - exact shift/mask is observed.
pub unsafe fn fn_8019f1a0() -> u32 {
    ((read_u16(AUDIO_DMA_BYTES_LEFT) & 0x7FFF) as u32) << 5
}

/// Reconstructs the audio DMA's main-memory source address.
///
/// The address is split across two 16-bit registers: bits 16..28 come from
/// `0xCC005030 & 0x1FFF`, bits 5..15 from `0xCC005032 & 0xFFE0`. The writer
/// `0x8019F100` drops the new address's bits 0..4 and keeps the old register's
/// bits 13..15 (address bits 29..31); this reader masks both groups off, so
/// they come back as zero. The result keeps only address bits 5..28.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_8019f1b0` at `0x8019f1b0` (24 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/8019f1b0_FUN_8019f1b0.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// None.
///
/// # Returns
/// Source address with bits 0..4 clear.
///
/// # Side effects
/// None; MMIO read-only.
///
/// # Safety
/// Only meaningful on hardware/emulator with the DSP register block mapped.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - exact shift/mask is observed.
pub unsafe fn fn_8019f1b0() -> u32 {
    (((read_u16(AUDIO_DMA_START_HI) & 0x1FFF) as u32) << 16)
        | ((read_u16(AUDIO_DMA_START_LO) & 0xFFE0) as u32)
}

/// Returns the programmed audio DMA length in bytes.
///
/// The control/length register stores the length in 32-byte blocks in bits
/// 0..14 and the start bit in bit 15; shifting left by five yields bytes.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_8019f1d0` at `0x8019f1d0` (16 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/8019f1d0_FUN_8019f1d0.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// None.
///
/// # Returns
/// Length in bytes, i.e. `(0xCC005036 & 0x7FFF) << 5`.
///
/// # Side effects
/// None; MMIO read-only.
///
/// # Safety
/// Only meaningful on hardware/emulator with the DSP register block mapped.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - exact shift/mask is observed.
pub unsafe fn fn_8019f1d0() -> u32 {
    ((read_u16(AUDIO_DMA_CONTROL_LEN) & 0x7FFF) as u32) << 5
}

// ===========================================================================
// Wave 3 constants - AI/DSP and JAS driver
//
// Every r13-relative global is written as `SDA1_BASE - offset`. The offsets
// are the signed displacements printed by Ghidra; no symbol table survives in
// the image, so the names only describe the observed role.
// ===========================================================================

/// `r13 - 0x5190` - AI/DSP interrupt callback word set by [`fn_8019f0b0`].
const SDA_IRQ_CALLBACK: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x5190;
/// `r13 - 0x5194` - argument handed to that callback (0 means "no argument").
const SDA_IRQ_CALLBACK_ARG: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x5194;
/// `r13 - 0x51C4` - re-entrancy guard for the DSP interrupt callback.
const SDA_IRQ_IN_CALLBACK: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x51C4;
/// `r13 - 0x51C8` - one after AI/DSP bring-up, read by [`fn_8019f1e0`].
const SDA_AI_INIT_DONE: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x51C8;
/// `r13 - 0x61E0` - pointer word read and passed to `0x801AB1C0` during init.
/// `// unknown:` the target of the pointer is not recovered.
const SDA_AI_VERSION_PTR: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x61E0;

/// `r13 - 0x519C` - first of five word-sized timing/scale values computed
/// from the bus clock. Used as a float by `0x8019EDB0`.
/// `// unknown:` the unit is not recovered; see [`fn_8019f1f0`].
const SDA_AI_SCALE_0: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x519C;
/// `r13 - 0x51A0` - zeroed companion of [`SDA_AI_SCALE_0`].
const SDA_AI_SCRATCH_0: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x51A0;
/// `r13 - 0x51A4` - second timing/scale word.
const SDA_AI_SCALE_1: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x51A4;
/// `r13 - 0x51A8` - zeroed companion of [`SDA_AI_SCALE_1`].
const SDA_AI_SCRATCH_1: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x51A8;
/// `r13 - 0x51AC` - third timing/scale word.
const SDA_AI_SCALE_2: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x51AC;
/// `r13 - 0x51B0` - zeroed companion of [`SDA_AI_SCALE_2`].
const SDA_AI_SCRATCH_2: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x51B0;
/// `r13 - 0x51B4` - fourth timing/scale word.
const SDA_AI_SCALE_3: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x51B4;
/// `r13 - 0x51B8` - zeroed companion of [`SDA_AI_SCALE_3`].
const SDA_AI_SCRATCH_3: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x51B8;
/// `r13 - 0x51BC` - fifth timing/scale word.
const SDA_AI_SCALE_4: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x51BC;
/// `r13 - 0x51C0` - zeroed companion of [`SDA_AI_SCALE_4`].
const SDA_AI_SCRATCH_4: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x51C0;

/// `r13 - 0x5140` - JAS callback slot A (set by [`fn_801a0ca0`]).
const SDA_JAS_CB_A: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x5140;
/// `r13 - 0x5144` - JAS callback slot B.
const SDA_JAS_CB_B: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x5144;
/// `r13 - 0x5148` - JAS callback slot C.
const SDA_JAS_CB_C: usize = SDA1_BASE - 0x5148;
/// `r13 - 0x514C` - argument paired with callback slot A.
