// ===========================================================================
// Wave 90 - the JAS DSP block pump, command builder and voice kernels
// (`0x801A08F0..0x801A93F0`).
//
// This band is the part of the audio driver that turns the mixed voice state
// into DSP command blocks and runs the per-voice effect kernels. It sits on top
// of the wave-5 voice/buffer control code and under the wave-3 AI/DSP bring-up.
//
// The three `0x801A` sub-bands are:
//
// 1. the three-bank DSP block pump and its dirty-bank bookkeeping
//    (`0x801A08F0`, `0x801A0E90`, `0x801A2E70`);
// 2. the per-voice parameter/curve builders (`0x801A3290`, `0x801A4180`,
//    `0x801A5280`, `0x801A73E0`);
// 3. the 2-, 3- and 4-channel 8-bit and 16-bit resampler kernels
//    (`0x801A3880`, `0x801A48F0`, `0x801A5C10`, `0x801A6580`, `0x801A6D30`,
//    `0x801A7A80`, `0x801A8150`, `0x801A88B0`, `0x801A93F0`).
//
// `r2` supplies the unnamed float/double constants of the kernels; they stay
// `SDA2_BASE - N` with their role inferred from the surrounding comparisons.
//
// The `0x802DCD58..0x802DCD74` prologues are CodeWarrior register-save thunks,
// not functions: the object pointer Ghidra prints as their return value is the
// incoming `r3`, so the bodies below take it explicitly.
// ===========================================================================

